1. **A computer-implemented method for controlling a trajectory of a machine tool**, the method comprising:  

   generating, based on a model of a machining environment, an initial collision-free trajectory between a starting configuration and a target configuration, the initial collision-free trajectory comprising a plurality of rectilinear trajectory segments;  

   generating an improved trajectory from the initial collision-free trajectory by replacing at least portions of two or more of the rectilinear trajectory segments with at least one polynomial trajectory segment;  

   performing collision detection on the improved trajectory using the model of the machining environment;  

   in response to detecting a collision along the improved trajectory, modifying the model, regenerating the improved trajectory based on the modified model, and repeating the collision detection, model modification, and trajectory regeneration until a collision-free improved trajectory is obtained; and  

   controlling relative movement between a tool and a workpiece according to the collision-free improved trajectory.

1. **A computer-implemented method for improving a non-cutting trajectory of a tool of a machine tool, the method comprising:**

   receiving an initial non-cutting trajectory comprising a plurality of linear trajectory segments extending between a departure point and an arrival point;

   generating, based on the initial non-cutting trajectory, a polynomial trajectory extending between the departure point and the arrival point;

   optimizing coefficients of the polynomial trajectory subject to a travel-envelope model representing a permitted region of travel of the tool;

   performing one or more collision checks on the optimized polynomial trajectory using a digital representation of the tool and at least one object in a workspace of the machine tool;

   in response to determining that the optimized polynomial trajectory fails at least one of the one or more collision checks, modifying the travel-envelope model based on a location associated with the failed collision check and repeating the optimizing of the coefficients using the modified travel-envelope model; and

   controlling the machine tool to move the tool along a collision-free polynomial trajectory produced by the repeated optimizing while the tool is not cutting a workpiece.
